<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Jesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1593–1596</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is a fortress built by the Portuguese at Mombasa between 1593 and 1596 to guard their trade on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast. Designed by the Italian architect Giovanni Battista Cairati, it changed hands repeatedly: Omani forces of the Ya'ariba dynasty took it in 1698 after a siege of nearly three years, and it later served the Mazrui governors of Mombasa. It is now a museum and a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-jesus.jpg" alt="Fort Jesus" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maingi030 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Jesus_at_the_Mombasa_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
